Functions
What is a Function?
A function is a named block of code that performs a specific task and can be called (invoked) from elsewhere in the program.
func()  ←─── function call
func()  ←─── function definition
Parts of a Function
void func()          ← function definition header
{
    stmt 2;
    stmt 3;
}

Example 1 — Basic Function Call
cvoid test() {
    printf("Hello");
}

void main() {
    test();     // function call
}
// O/P: Hello
Execution flow:
main() ──→ [memory] ──→ test()
           ←──────────────────

Example 2 — Chained Function Calls
cvoid dept() {
    printf("I met Mentor\n");
}

void shortBreak() {
    printf("I had coffee\n");
    dept();
    printf("I came back to shortBreak\n");
}

void main() {
    printf("I came to College\n");
    shortBreak();
    printf("I came back to College\n");
}
Output:
I came to College
I had coffee
I met mentor
I came back to short
I came back to college

Purpose / Why Use Functions?

Code reusing — write once, use many times.
Functions can be defined in any order, but compilation is always top to bottom; execution always starts from main().
When a function call is executed, control goes to that function's definition and executes it.
As soon as the function definition is completed, control returns to the function call place.
A function can be called any number of times.
Calling a function with less or more arguments than defined will result in a compilation error.


Example 3 — Function with Parameters
cvoid Bday(int x, float y) {
    printf("%d %.f", x, y);
}

void main() {
    int a = 5;
    float b = 0.5;
    Bday(a, b);     // O/P: 5 0.5
}
Calling variations and errors:
cBday(5, 2.5);       // O/P: 5 25
Bday('a', 'b');     // O/P: 97 98
Bday(3.5, 2.5);     // O/P: 2 25  (implicit truncation)
Bday(5/2, 5/2);     // O/P: 2 20
Bday(10, 2013.0);   // O/P: CE (compilation error - too many args maybe)
Bday();             // CE (Bday missing args)
